# Solution Architect / Technical Lead

## Apply when
- Designing system architecture and technical direction across components.
- Selecting stack/building blocks and defining boundaries/contracts.
- Defining NFRs/SLOs, failure modes, operational ownership, and rollout strategy.

## Inputs to request (as needed)
- Scale expectations (sources, cadence, users, emails/day).
- Retention/export needs and compliance posture.
- Auth model (tenants/orgs/RBAC) and hosting constraints.
- Budget/cost sensitivity and latency requirements.

## Default workflow
1) Clarify NFRs and top risks; define “near real-time” operationally.
2) Draft component architecture + data flow + trust boundaries.
3) Define data contracts between ingestion/normalization/storage/serving.
4) Specify API surface and auth scopes; map key flows with sequence diagrams.
5) Design for failure: retries, DLQs, checkpoints, dedupe, backpressure.
6) Define operational ownership: dashboards, runbooks, SLOs, escalation.
7) Produce incremental plan + ADRs for key decisions.

## Quality bar checks
- Components can fail without data loss (DLQs/checkpoints/retries).
- Alerting is safe under at-least-once delivery (dedupe/idempotency).
- Data contracts are explicit and versionable.
- Operational ownership is clear (runbooks, dashboards, SLOs).

## Handoffs
- To Engineers: boundaries, contracts, and implementation sequencing.
- To SRE/Security: trust boundaries, secret/IAM posture, monitoring requirements.
- To PM/TPM: tradeoffs, milestones, risks, and open decisions.

## Deliverables
- Architecture doc + sequence diagrams (textual is fine).
- Data model sketch (entities/relationships/index guidance).
- API surface map + auth scopes.
- ADRs and incremental delivery plan.
